WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
I hope everybody who had a long&nbsp;
weekend had a good long weekend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.880
We are back and we must dove right into it.
Last week, I laid out for you one framework&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.800
for looking at the polling that was relatively&nbsp;
optimistic. And that framework essentially was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.920
Joe Biden is beating Trump nationally by more&nbsp;
than Hillary was beating Trump nationally at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:28.720
this point four years ago. And Joe Biden is doing&nbsp;
quite well in some important swing states. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.600
I want to give you an alternative perspective,&nbsp;
which gives us reason for caution and for care.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.520
And it's a different framing of the same data&nbsp;
and in the states likely to be decision makers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:46.080
The reality is that Joe Biden is not doing&nbsp;
as well as Hillary Clinton was doing at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.680
point in 2016. Now, let me be very clear as we get&nbsp;
into this. I'm not telling you which framework is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:57.280
correct. Ultimately, there is a reason why we do&nbsp;
the voting. We don't just say these are the polls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:04.000
So that's the election. The election is the actual&nbsp;
voting. And this is merely a point of perspective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:09.280
It can be at the same time true that Joe Biden&nbsp;
is doing better nationally and in some states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:15.680
than Hillary was in 2016 while trailing in other&nbsp;
states relative to Hillary. It doesn't tell us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.240
who will win. Only are voting, tells us who and&nbsp;
who will win. So the conclusion is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:25.920
we have to vote. But let's look at the data.&nbsp;
We're going to compare three important states,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.400
Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. Those&nbsp;
are three states that Trump won in 2016.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:35.680
And we're going to look at how Hillary was&nbsp;
doing. Twenty two days before the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.560
compared to how Biden is doing. So let's start&nbsp;
in Michigan. In Michigan, at this point in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.880
Hillary was winning by ten point seven.&nbsp;
While today Biden is winning by only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.920
seven. So Hillary's lead at this point in&nbsp;
Michigan was bigger than Biden's today. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.960
what happened in the end in Michigan? Trump&nbsp;
won by zero point three. In Pennsylvania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.600
we had Joe Biden. Today, we have Joe Biden&nbsp;
today with essentially the same lead that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:12.080
Hillary had in 2016. It's a difference of less&nbsp;
than half a point. And in the end, in 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:19.040
Donald Trump won in Pennsylvania by just&nbsp;
under one point. Then we have Wisconsin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:27.680
And in Wisconsin, Joe Biden currently leads by six&nbsp;
point one. That is a smaller lead than Hillary's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:36.800
at this point in 2016. And again, that is a state&nbsp;
that ultimately was won by Donald Trump. Why does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:44.480
this matter? It matters because even if Biden gets&nbsp;
a larger popular vote victory than Donald Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.600
on the wings of massive victories in large blue&nbsp;
states like California, New York, as examples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.440
he can still lose electorally if he loses these&nbsp;
states. Case in point, if we look at average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:06.720
polling right now and look at a sort of consensus&nbsp;
map in which we give Joe Biden, Nevada and North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.920
Carolina, which we don't know, that Biden will&nbsp;
win, but give Biden Nevada, give Biden North&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.160
Carolina. And we simply leave out for the moment.&nbsp;
The three states we're talking about and Florida.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:28.480
That gets Joe Biden 257 electoral votes. That's 13&nbsp;
shy of the number he needs. If in that scenario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.400
Biden wins Michigan or Pennsylvania or any&nbsp;
two of the three states, Michigan, Wisconsin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:41.680
Pennsylvania, then Biden no longer needs Florida&nbsp;
at all. And Biden wins. But if Joe Biden ends up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:45.840
losing these three states that we're talking&nbsp;
about, Wisconsin, Michigan and Pennsylvania,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.200
all of a sudden the entire election&nbsp;
comes down to Florida and Florida alone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.920
which did not go well in 2016 for Hillary&nbsp;
Clinton. Now, of course, you can say, come on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:03.280
David. The polling won't be wrong in all of&nbsp;
those states. I certainly hope not. But remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.080
the polling tightened in the last&nbsp;
two weeks. The exercise today is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:15.600
if we look at Biden today in Wisconsin, Michigan&nbsp;
and Pennsylvania, compared to Hillary 22 days out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:21.760
four years ago, Biden is not doing as well as&nbsp;
Hillary was doing at that point. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.760
the polling tightened. So the point here is&nbsp;
when it's this close, the confidence does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:34.160
nothing for us. We just have to know that&nbsp;
these states look okay for Biden right now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.920
They looked better for Hillary in 2016 and she&nbsp;
lost those states and subsequently the election,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.800
which is why our conclusion from all of this&nbsp;
beautiful academic exercise here. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:51.840
conclusion is we can do nothing other than vote&nbsp;
in every state as if it's 50/50. And our vote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:59.840
my vote, your vote could be the vote that decides&nbsp;
your state. If you game it out. Is the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.440
rational approach to this election. I hope that&nbsp;
is the approach you are going to be up adopting.

